=begin pod

=NAME Duallity

=SUBTITLE Composable fuzzy-query automata over Vinary Tree dictionaries

=DESCRIPTION

C<Duallity> captures an immutable Unicode dictionary revision as a lazy
weighted finite-state transducer. The returned
C<Vinary::Tree::Interop::Wfst> composes directly with native and host-defined
C<Lling::Llang> graphs.

=head1 Public API

=item C<abi-version> and C<api-revision> report the loaded native contract.

=item C<wfst($dictionary, $query, :maximum-distance, :algorithm, :kind)>
returns one independently owned lazy graph. C<$dictionary> is a Vinary Tree
C<Resource> or C<Dictionary> implementing C<vt.dictionary.v1> with Unicode
scalar units.

=item C<Algorithm> provides C<STANDARD>, C<TRANSPOSITION>,
C<MERGE-AND-SPLIT>, and C<DAMERAU-LEVENSHTEIN>.

=item C<WfstKind> provides C<LEVENSHTEIN>, the three C<UNIVERSAL-*>, the four
C<GENERALIZED-*>, and C<FZF> variants. FZF uses Arctic weights; all others use
tropical weights.

=item C<default-options> returns a versioned revision-3 raw options record.
C<configured-wfst($dictionary, $query, $options, :keepalive)> returns an owned
C<ConfiguredWfst> holder. Its C<.graph> is the ordinary interop WFST; its
C<.options>, C<.cache-statistics>, C<.clear-cache>, and C<.set-cache-policy>
methods access the native configuration handle. C<.options> copies nested
operation text and limits before returning.

=head1 Example

=begin code :lang<raku>
use Duallity;
use Libdictenstein;
my $dictionary = dynamic-dawg;
$dictionary.insert-batch([cat => Nil, cot => Nil]);
my $view = $dictionary.snapshot;
my $graph = wfst($view, 'cat', maximum-distance => 1);
$view.close;
say $graph.arcs($graph.start).elems;
$graph.close;
$dictionary.close;
=end code

=head1 Configured graph

=begin code :lang<raku>
use Duallity;
use Libdictenstein;
my $dictionary = dynamic-dawg;
$dictionary.insert-batch([cat => Nil]);
my $view = $dictionary.snapshot;
my $options = default-options();
$options.cache-policy = LRU;
$options.cache-capacity = 64;
my $configured = configured-wfst($view, 'cat', $options);
say $configured.graph.start;
$configured.clear-cache;
say $configured.cache-statistics.clears;
$configured.close;
$view.close;
$dictionary.close;
=end code

Nested raw pointers require a C<keepalive> array holding every referenced
buffer and record through construction. The native constructor deep-copies
custom operation text. The generated C<Duallity::ConfigAbi> record classes
use writable native-width address slots for C pointers; callers must not use
C<CArray[Record]> as a contiguous C record array. See the packaged
C<README.md> and the source configuration mirror guide for exact limits.

=head1 Ownership and composition

Construction captures one immutable dictionary snapshot in constant time with
respect to dictionary size. Closing or mutating the source cannot alter the
graph. C<Lling::Llang::compose> retains both operands and expands only reachable
product states. Close every returned graph deterministically. The configured
holder owns a second native handle and closes both resources together.

=head1 Failure, concurrency, and security

Native failures throw C<X::Duallity> with a stable C<Status>, operation, and
copied diagnostic. Provider callbacks are capability- and domain-checked, page
validated, and serialized unless they truthfully advertise parallel
reentrancy. Treat a foreign provider as synchronous plugin code and constrain
its work.

See the packaged C<README.md> and C<t/01-conformance.rakutest> for every
selector, weight domain, troubleshooting case, benchmark, and executable
composition example.

=end pod
